 <name>scope</name>
 <channel>pecl.php.net</channel>
 <summary>Scope: the identity of the operation (request, job) that code belongs to</summary>
 <description>The Scope class proposed in the PHP RFC "Scope", for PHP 8.2 and later. Fibers and generators belong to the scope current at their creation; Scope::getRootScope() is the scope outside any Scope::run().</description>
 <lead>
  <name>Frode Børli</name>
  <user>frodeborli</user>
  <email>frode@ennerd.com</email>
  <active>yes</active>
 </lead>
 <date>2026-10-26</date>
 <version>
  <release>0.4.1</release>
  <api>0.4.0</api>
 </version>
 <stability>
  <release>alpha</release>
  <api>alpha</api>
 </stability>
 <license uri="https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT">MIT</license>
 <notes>0.4.1: a generator whose finally block ends with a yield no longer crashes when an exception or a return enters the block (finally_end was moved onto an inserted scope switch; found with php-src's test suite); the extension allocates no object until the first Scope, so object ids are as without it; a scope destroyed with its session active writes and closes it in the destructor phase, so user save handlers (session_set_save_handler()) run safely, also when the scope is collected as a cycle or is alive at request shutdown (fixes a use-after-free when ending the session released the scope); Scope reports its superglobals and session to the cycle collector; $_SESSION is per scope like the other superglobals (a new scope has none, and it keeps its array after session_write_close(), session_abort() and session_destroy(), as in a request).</notes>
